course: dictionary
lesson: 1973
title: Completely: all the way
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Completely means fully, totally, with nothing left out. A completely full glass has no room for more. When you see completely, ask: is anything missing? If the answer is no, completely is doing its job.
explain: Completely also makes another word stronger, as in completely different or completely right. «Gran's "never mix bleach with other cleaners" is completely true.» Completely true means it is true with no exceptions at all.
explain: The words around a word give you clues. «When a living thing dies, its body stops working completely and will not start again.» The clue is will not start again: the body does not stop a little, it stops all the way.
fact: Completely means fully or totally, with nothing left out.
fact: «Completely» makes a word stronger: completely different, completely right.
fact: «Venus is wrapped in thick clouds that hide its surface completely» means no part of the surface shows through.
fact: In maths, a completely full jug holds its whole capacity, so the total amount counts.
fact: A good check is to swap in fully or all the way and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «Many animals change shape completely as they grow up.» What does completely mean here? | Slowly over many years | *Totally, the whole body changes | Partly, just a little | Quietly
exercise: Nadia says a glass is completely full. What does that mean? | It is cold to touch | It is half full | *It has no room left, all of it is full | It is nearly empty
exercise: «Gran's rule is completely true.» Which word could replace completely without changing the meaning? | *Fully | Sometimes | Slightly | Nearly
test: «The ice melted completely in the sun.» What is the best meaning? | Only a bit melted | *All of it melted, none was left | It melted into a cloud | It froze again
test: Which sentence uses completely to make a word stronger? | *The sea was completely calm | The bird sang | Rain fell at dusk | He walked to school
exam: «Music in nature films can feel completely different, but the animals cannot hear it.» What does completely different mean? | Quieter | Louder | *Totally changed, not at all the same | Slightly changed
